I shall state their number, O brahmins; listen with devotion to all of it:
the Vidyeśa consists of ten thousand [verses], likewise the Rudra and the Vaināyaka;
the Auma and that named Mātṛpurāṇa have eight thousand each;
the Rudraikādaśaka contains thirteen thousand, O twice-born ones;

I instruct you especially, for you are dearest to me;
since you are a devotee of Viṣṇu, his devotee is exceedingly close to me.

The lord Rudra, the source of auspiciousness, instructed him repeatedly in this manner;
Nārada did not regard it as beneficial, deluded by the māyā of Śiva.

Kapālī with fifty million, the auspicious Sandāraka with sixty million, / and here Kaṇḍuka and Kuṇḍaka with tens of trillions;

Viṣṭambha went with eighty million, Candratāpana with eighty million;

the lord of the attendants Mahākeśa was surrounded by ten billion;

Brahmā said:
Having reflected thus, Śivā remembered that Maheśa in her mind;
then, having obtained Śiva's permission, Durgā said to me at that time:

Nārada said:
Proper is your speech, dear father, sinless one who knows all;
truly, the wonderful, auspicious story of the two Śivas has been heard by us.

Brahmā said:
Having thus spoken the rule of righteousness, she felt remorse;
she remembered the words of Śaṅkara with an aching heart.

Viṣṇu said:
"For those wishing to prosper, no [harm] has ever been committed against a more fiery one;
for those who have done wrong, the wish to prosper is for the sake of well-being in many ways.

Thus has been recounted the deed of Śambhu, his going forth, a supreme festival;
now listen, O Nārada, to the auspicious event that took place in the city of the Himālaya.

Thus ends the fortieth chapter, named “Description of Śiva’s Journey,” in the Pārvatīkhaṇḍa, the third section of the second Rudrasaṃhitā of the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.

The women of the city said:
"The eyes of the inhabitants of Himavat's city have borne fruit;
whoever has seen this form, his birth has indeed achieved its purpose.

Protector of the destitute, Maheśa, son of Śaṅkara, Lord of the three worlds, O Lord, Master of illusion, I have come for refuge; protect me, lover of Brahmins!
You are the Lord of all, praised by the gods headed by Brahmā who know all bows;
you assume an illusory form, granting joy to your devotees, intent on protection, user of illusion!

Then rivers flowed there by hundreds carrying blood; / bhūtas, pretas, and others gathered there by the hundreds.

Jackals and she-jackals devoured much flesh there, / as well as vultures, hawks, and meat-eating crows;

indeed, they ate the abundant flesh of the fallen. (30)

Śiva said:
This ruler of Tripura is indeed virtuous at present;
where virtue prevails, he must never be killed by the wise.

Sanatkumāra said:
Having heard these words, the gods fell into grief;
with their lotus-faces withered, they spoke again to Viṣṇu:

Sanatkumāra said:
Having spoken thus, the gods grieved again and again;
they found no rest nor could they proceed from the presence of the supreme deity.

Maheśvara said:
O bull among Dānavas, blessed are you, devoted to me and free from perturbations;
all those boons desired by you have now been granted to you.

Viṣṇu and the others said:
You must never fear at all, O shaven-headed ones;
this entire excellent act was done by the command of Śiva.

Sanatkumāra said:
Having spoken thus, [Brahmā] summoned Śukra and consecrated him to the kingship;
having taken leave of the lord of rivers, Brahmā vanished.

Hearing that, the blessed Rudra displayed vehement wrath;
he became terrifying and exceedingly dreadful, blazing through the quarters.

Thus ends the twentieth chapter, titled 'Description of the General Battle between the Attendants and the Asuras,' in the story of the slaying of Jalandhara, in the fifth Yuddhakhaṇḍa of the second Rudrasaṃhitā, in the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.

Hiraṇyakaśipu said:
O blessed Lord, ruler of creatures, O Grandfather, O god, let there never be fear of death for me
from weapons, missiles, nooses, thunderbolts, dried trees, great mountains, water, fire, or the strikes of enemies;

Saying nothing at all, the protector of the bhūtas tossed that foremost of poets into his mouth like a fruit;
and a great cry of distress—'Alas! Alas! Alas!'—was raised loudly by all those asuras.

Thus ends the forty-seventh chapter, entitled 'The Description of the Swallowing of Śukra in the War with Andhaka,' in the War Section, the fifth section of the second saṃhitā, the Rudrasaṃhitā, in the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.

Nandīśvara said:
Countless indeed are the incarnations across the kalpas of the all-pervading Lord of all;
nevertheless, I will speak of them here according to my intellect.

Śakra said:
"O seeker of a son, I will give you a son born of a womb, subject to death;
otherwise, I will not give one to you, for beings free from death do not exist.

The son said:
Not by austerity, O father, shall I cheat death, nor by knowledge;
by worshipping Mahādeva I shall conquer death, not otherwise.

Umā said:
You should grant [this] to Nandin, O God of gods, O Parameśvara;
most exceedingly dear to me, O Lord, is this son of Śalāda.

Nandīśvara said:
When he was addressed thus, Śaṅkara, the lord of all, said, “So be it”;
and while he was watching, the blessed Hara vanished right there.

The people said:
“O masters, what is to be done? The wicked ones have arrived;
many people have been harmed, and they have come near!”

The command, “Kill them, strike them!” was spoken by Dūṣaṇa;
that word uttered by the Daitya was not heard at all by those twice-born,
the sons of Vedapriya, who were devoted to the path of meditation on Śambhu.

Thus this eulogy of Vaidyanātheśvara has been proclaimed;
for people who hear it, sin is turned to ashes.

Thus ends the twenty-eighth chapter, titled 'Description of the Eulogy of the Vaidyanātheśvara Jyotirliṅga,' in the fourth Koṭirudrasaṃhitā of the glorious Śiva Mahāpurāṇa.

Sūta said:
Hearing those words of hers, the rākṣasas became joyful;
all those rākṣasas, now fearless, said to one another then:

Śrī Kṛṣṇa said:
O god of gods, O Mahādeva, I indeed ask for the best of boons
from you, numbering eight, as indicated by you, O lord, O great lord:

occupying a supreme celestial vehicle, moving everywhere, granting all desires, / surrounded by the maidens of Rudra for him who is detached from the branches;

delightful with the sound of dancing, singing, mṛdaṅgas, and other musical instruments, / adorned with divine garments, garlands, unguents, and ornaments;

filled with jars of divine nectar, brimming with divine water, / resembling ten million suns, very cool like ten million moons;

and having granted the celestial vehicle, swift as thought and going everywhere— / when his curiosity for pleasure is exhausted even after he has enjoyed pleasures,

causing his very fierce power to descend, which is naturally exceedingly difficult to attain, / wishing to burn down the forest [of saṃsāra], having the splendid radiance of the fire of dissolution;

having bestowed the import of the great mantra, Parameśvara [confers the realization]: / 'I am complete,' consisting in the constant contemplation, 'I am Śambhu.'

And having granted samādhi and unhindered supernatural powers, / which take the form of the vibration of devotion to himself and the ability to perform the work of the sun and others;

at the end of the life of the Lotus-born [Brahmā], the guru of the world / bestows upon him supreme liberation, devoid of return.

They are all without possessions, yet devoted to sharing;
they are eaters, yet devoid of conduct; they are proclaimed as spirits and the like.
The first creation of Brahmā, the Supreme Deity, is that of the Mahat;
the second is that of the subtle elements, which is called the elemental creation.

having gratified their own father, the Supreme Lord, through tapas,
and having attained his grace with their whole being from the Supreme Deity:
in an earlier kalpa, Rudra created Brahmā and Nārāyaṇa;
in another kalpa again, Brahmā, whose nature is the universe, created Rudra and Viṣṇu;

restrained himself from the creation of offspring and remained with the Rudras.
From then on, that god does not produce auspicious offspring;
with his seed drawn upward, he stood as Sthāṇu until the dissolution of beings.

